  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Kansas received $2.6B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state logged 20,091 awards with an average award size of $129K, and obligations fell 27.5% year over year. These totals are partial because fiscal year to date figures are not full-year totals. Year-by-year obligations show prior fiscal years at $2.9B in FY21, $4.2B in FY22, $3B in FY23, $3.3B in FY24, and $3.6B in FY25. The current FY26 total of $2.6B places the state below several recent fiscal years. Concentration is notable: one agency and one industry each account for roughly half or more of the statewide obligations. Buyers range from large civilian health and transportation agencies to smaller program offices, and suppliers run from major systems integrators and avionics firms to couriers and construction contractors. These figures reflect awarded obligations only and do not show unspent balances, future options, or anticipated modifications beyond the fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies drive the most federal spending in Kansas?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs is the largest buyer in Kansas with $1.5B in obligations. The Department of Defense and the Department of Transportation follow with $470M and $455M respectively. Smaller buyers include the Department of Agriculture at $105M and the Environmental Protection Agency at $37.4M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors capture the majority of obligations in Kansas?",
        "body": "Computer Systems Design Services is the largest industry with $1.3B in obligations. ORACLE HEALTH GOVERNMENT SERVICES, INC. is the top vendor with $1.3B, followed by INDRA AIR TRAFFIC, INC. at $374M and TEXTRON AVIATION INC at $83.2M. Other significant industry categories include Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $373M and Aircraft Manufacturing at $91.1M."
      }
    ]
  },
